From: EasyStick maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sdio: acknowledge the slave interrupt instead of echoing it

esp_handle_isr() writes the value it just read from INT_ST back to INT_CLR.
INT_CLR is write-1-to-clear, so when the read returns zero the write
acknowledges nothing.  dw_mmc masks its per-slot SDIO bit inside the hard
IRQ handler and re-arms it from ack_sdio_irq() once the callback returns, so
an unacknowledged slave assertion re-enters this callback immediately and
without bound.  On the experimental ESP32-P4 host port that closed loop is
what produces the 254 identical "status=0x00000000" callbacks per boot, and
it also reconciles the two register readings that looked contradictory:
RINTSTS keeps bit 17 latched while a one-shot MINTSTS sample lands inside
the masked window and reads zero.

Three changes, all confined to the interrupt path:

  - Acknowledge with the named INT bits when INT_ST reads zero (or the read
    itself fails), so the host can always break the loop.  Dispatch still
    uses the value actually read, so a forced acknowledge cannot fabricate
    an RX_NEW_PACKET event.

  - Split the early-return guard.  sdio_claim_irq() arms this callback in
    esp_probe() well before the adapter reaches ESP_CONTEXT_RX_READY, and
    every interrupt taken in that window was previously dropped without
    writing INT_CLR -- the same unbounded loop, entered earlier.  The slave
    is now acknowledged whenever a context exists; only dispatch waits for
    the adapter.

  - Sample INT_RAW at the head of each empty-status streak.  A raw latch
    that INT_ST does not report means the slave never enabled that source
    for host delivery, which is a slave-side configuration fault rather
    than a missing frame.  This is the measurement the previous telemetry
    could not make, because every sample it took was of the loop.

The empty-status log is rate limited to the first four of a streak and then
every 256th, so a fault that survives this patch stays legible on the
console instead of flooding it.  Board-port telemetry only: register values
and counts, never payload contents or Wi-Fi credentials.

What this does not fix: if DAT1 is held by a source outside the bits
esp_sdio_decl.h names, the forced acknowledge cannot clear it and the loop
persists -- the INT_RAW sample is there to identify exactly that case.

Verified on hardware 2026-08-11, cold Type-C power cycle, read-only 60 s
capture (easystick-p4-israck-boot-20260811.log, sha256
85994ab595eee6a386b20a85ced7e9f90b13be8642a987909f0fceaf30ed6227):

  - The loop stops.  254 distinct callbacks became 2, both inside the first
    300 ms after OPEN_DATA_PATH, and zero after "ESP SDIO probe completed".
    The pre-patch capture ended with the storm still running; this one runs
    to its natural end.  Not rate limiting: the streak reached 1, and the
    first four of a streak always print.  Not an unloaded driver: no
    rmmod/unload and no Oops, BUG, panic or WARNING in the log.

  - INT_RAW is genuinely zero, not unreadable.  Both records read
    "raw=0x00000000 raw_ret=0" with "st_ret=0", so the reads succeeded and
    the zeros are values.  The slave-side interrupt configuration fault this
    sample was added to detect is therefore refuted rather than confirmed:
    no interrupt event exists at the source.

  - The forced acknowledge destroyed nothing.  0010's independent PACKET_LEN
    poll read len=0 ret=0 on all ten iterations across 2.7 s, plus one
    CMD53 read at len=0 ret=0, and that path performs no INT_CLR write.

  - Note when comparing counts: the console carries keep_bootcon, so every
    line after "legacy console [ttyGS1] enabled" appears twice.  The 508
    figure in earlier notes and in the in-source comment below is a raw grep
    count of that doubled stream.  The comment is left as written because
    changing it would desynchronize this patch from the source that produced
    the capture above; read it as 254 distinct.

  - The software delta measured against was 0010 + 0011 + the esp32_sdio
    resetpin default, not 0011 alone.

Still unmeasured: this patch's INT_CLR path has only ever been exercised
against an all-zero status, because the C6 has never asserted an interrupt.

---
 sdio/esp_sdio.c | 70 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 64 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

--- a/sdio/esp_sdio.c
+++ b/sdio/esp_sdio.c
@@ -63,10 +63,27 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Union of every SLCHOST interrupt source this driver's own register map
+ * names.  INT_CLR is write-1-to-clear, so this is the widest acknowledge
+ * that can be justified from esp_sdio_decl.h alone.
+ */
+#define ESP_SLAVE_INT_CLR_KNOWN	(ESP_SLAVE_BIT0_INT | ESP_SLAVE_BIT1_INT |	\
+				 ESP_SLAVE_BIT2_INT | ESP_SLAVE_BIT3_INT |	\
+				 ESP_SLAVE_BIT4_INT | ESP_SLAVE_BIT5_INT |	\
+				 ESP_SLAVE_BIT6_INT | ESP_SLAVE_BIT7_INT |	\
+				 ESP_SLAVE_RX_UNDERFLOW_INT |			\
+				 ESP_SLAVE_TX_OVERFLOW_INT |			\
+				 ESP_SLAVE_RX_NEW_PACKET_INT)
+
 static void esp_handle_isr(struct sdio_func *func)
 {
+	/* SDIO IRQ context only, one function at a time: no locking needed. */
+	static u32 empty_isr_streak;
 	struct esp_sdio_context *context = NULL;
 	u32 *int_status;
+	u32 status_read;
+	bool dispatch;
 	int ret;
 
 	if (!func) {
@@ -78,12 +95,23 @@
 
 	context = sdio_get_drvdata(func);
 
-	if (!(context) ||
-	    !(context->adapter) ||
-	    (atomic_read(&context->adapter->state) < ESP_CONTEXT_RX_READY)) {
+	if (!context) {
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * An interrupt taken before the adapter reaches ESP_CONTEXT_RX_READY
+	 * must still be acknowledged.  sdio_claim_irq() arms this callback in
+	 * esp_probe() well before that state is set, and dw_mmc masks its
+	 * per-slot SDIO bit inside the hard IRQ handler and re-arms it from
+	 * ack_sdio_irq() after every callback.  Returning without writing
+	 * INT_CLR therefore leaves the slave asserting DAT1 and this callback
+	 * re-enters immediately, forever.  Acknowledge the slave in every
+	 * case; only dispatch work when the adapter can accept it.
+	 */
+	dispatch = context->adapter &&
+		   atomic_read(&context->adapter->state) >= ESP_CONTEXT_RX_READY;
+
 	int_status = kmalloc(sizeof(u32), GFP_ATOMIC);
 
 	if (!int_status) {
@@ -94,10 +122,40 @@
 	ret = esp_read_reg(context, ESP_SLAVE_INT_ST_REG,
 			(u8 *) int_status, sizeof(*int_status), ACQUIRE_LOCK);
 	CHECK_SDIO_RW_ERROR(ret);
-	esp_info("SDIO IRQ received: read_ret=%d status=0x%08x\n",
-		 ret, *int_status);
 
-	esp_process_interrupt(context, *int_status);
+	status_read = ret ? 0 : *int_status;
+
+	if (dispatch)
+		esp_process_interrupt(context, status_read);
+
+	if (status_read) {
+		esp_info("SDIO IRQ received: status=0x%08x dispatch=%d after_empty=%u\n",
+			 status_read, dispatch, empty_isr_streak);
+		empty_isr_streak = 0;
+		*int_status = status_read;
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * Writing back the zero that was just read acknowledges
+		 * nothing, so the slave keeps DAT1 asserted and this callback
+		 * spins -- the 508 identical "status=0x00000000" callbacks per
+		 * boot recorded on this port are that spin, not slave noise.
+		 * Force the named bits down instead, and sample INT_RAW at the
+		 * head of each streak: a raw latch that INT_ST does not report
+		 * means the slave never enabled that source for host delivery,
+		 * which is a slave-side configuration fault rather than a
+		 * missing frame.
+		 */
+		if (empty_isr_streak < 4 || !(empty_isr_streak % 256)) {
+			u32 raw = 0;
+			int raw_ret = esp_read_reg(context, ESP_SLAVE_INT_RAW_REG,
+					(u8 *) &raw, sizeof(raw), ACQUIRE_LOCK);
+
+			esp_info("SDIO IRQ empty: streak=%u st_ret=%d raw=0x%08x raw_ret=%d dispatch=%d\n",
+				 empty_isr_streak, ret, raw, raw_ret, dispatch);
+		}
+		empty_isr_streak++;
+		*int_status = ESP_SLAVE_INT_CLR_KNOWN;
+	}
 
 	/* Clear interrupt status */
 	ret = esp_write_reg(context, ESP_SLAVE_INT_CLR_REG,
-- 
2.43.0
